{"answer":"The closest comparisons are legal dense-retrieval studies. On Brazilian legal search, legal-only fine-tuning favors specialized legal tasks, while mixing legal data with general question-answer supervision offers a stronger balance across legal and out-of-domain search [2]. For multilingual EU environmental legislation, contrastive fine-tuning in monolingual and bilingual settings improves retrieval over baselines, with gains transferring to unseen languages [1]. A Brazilian benchmark also finds domain-adaptation gains clearest on the supervision-aligned subset, with other retrieval approaches remaining competitive across collections [6]. These papers compare retrieval utility across legal domains and settings; they do not provide a single controlled comparison of fine-tuning methods across diverse legal technical-document domains.","grounded":true} 
